// Google Batch / Seqera Platform profile.
//
// WHY beforeScript SYMLINKS projectDir
//
// The pipeline invokes `pixi run --frozen --manifest-path ${projectDir}/pixi.toml ...`, which
// assumes the pinned pixi environment -- built once, at image-build time, via
// `pixi install && pixi run setup` -- lives on disk at whatever path Nextflow resolves for
// `${projectDir}` at runtime. On Sherlock that is true by construction: the repo is checked out
// once onto shared NFS (see conf/sherlock.config). On Google Batch, Nextflow instead stages the
// pipeline source fresh into a container-specific
// `/.nextflow/assets/.repos/<org>/<repo>/clones/<sha>` path that has nothing to do with where the
// image's baked environment actually lives (`/opt/pipeline`, per the Dockerfile used to build the
// image below). Without this fix, every task fails with `pixi: command not found` -- confirmed
// directly, 2026-08-26 -- because the projectDir pixi ever sees has no environment in it.
//
// beforeScript replaces the task's projectDir with a symlink to the image's baked copy before the
// task script runs, so `pixi run` finds the environment without the task needing its own network
// access or its own multi-minute `pixi install`. Two things about it are load-bearing:
//
//   - `${projectDir}` is not materialized inside the task container until something touches it --
//     Fusion mounts it lazily -- so `mkdir -p $(dirname ...)` forces that before the `ln`, or the
//     symlink silently fails and the task falls through to the same `pixi: command not found`.
//   - Every step is `|| true`. A `beforeScript` that itself errors aborts the task before the real
//     command runs, which reports as a bare "process terminated with an error exit status" with no
//     indication the failure was in setup rather than in the pipeline.
//
// DO NOT RUN THIS PROFILE LOCALLY. `${projectDir}` is only a throwaway per-task clone on Google
// Batch. Run locally (executor = local, the default outside a Batch task), it resolves to the
// actual checked-out repo, and `rm -rf ${projectDir}` deletes it for real -- this destroyed the
// working tree once already, while testing this exact file. To sanity-check this profile's config
// resolves correctly without running any process, use `nextflow config -profile gcb .`, never
// `-stub-run` or a real run, outside of an actual Google Batch task context.
//
// WHY THERE IS NO google{}/wave{}/fusion{} BLOCK HERE -- EXCEPT batch.spot
//
// Most of google{} comes from the Seqera Platform compute environment attached at launch
// (confirmed by diffing what was actually passed in at launch -- just `process.container` and
// `process.beforeScript` -- against the effective config Seqera reported back, which additionally
// carried `executor = 'google-batch'`, `google { project; location; batch { ... } }`,
// `wave.enabled`, `fusion.enabled` and `workDir`). Hard-coding those here would duplicate
// organization-specific config that belongs to the compute environment, not to this pipeline, and
// would go stale the moment the compute environment changes.
//
// `batch.spot` is different: it is a fact about the *workload*, not about the account it happens
// to run in, so it belongs in the pipeline's own config rather than only in one compute
// environment's settings. This pipeline already tolerates preemption without help -- every
// process's `errorStrategy` (conf/base.config) retries on exit 143, which is what a preempted
// Google Batch task returns, and this was exercised for real: three tasks were preempted and
// retried automatically during the 2026-08-26 production Nextflow-vs-sbatch comparison run
// (job 38978285; see docs/status.md), no intervention needed. Nothing here trades power estimates
// for cost -- a preempted task simply reruns -- so there is no reason to ever pay full price.
// Setting it here means a differently-configured compute environment can't silently make this
// pipeline run at on-demand prices; today's compute environment (`hgrm-cloud-batch-us-central-WIF`)
// already sets it too, so this is belt-and-suspenders, not a change in behaviour.
//
// THE CONTAINER IMAGE HAS NO DEFAULT, DELIBERATELY
//
// `wave.seqera.io/wt/503b3282d5b9/wave/build:8b888ed3bf47e1ea` is the image that this profile's
// design was validated against (full pipeline run, all 7 processes, 2026-08-26) -- but it was built
// via Wave's ephemeral (non-frozen) mode, which expires roughly 24h after the build completes. It
// is not safe to bake in as a default: a run months from now would fail on a stale/deleted image
// with no clue why.
//
// The recipe is `containers/Dockerfile`. This comment previously pointed at "docs/status.md's
// Nextflow section for the exact Dockerfile", which never contained one -- the recipe was not
// recorded anywhere until 2026-09-04.
//
// Build a durable replacement with Wave's `--freeze` and a `--build-repo` that Seqera has push
// credentials for, and pass the result via `--container_image`. An ephemeral build is fine for a
// one-off validation and useless for a published result.
//
// REBUILD WHENEVER pixi.lock CHANGES. Nothing enforces it, and a stale image fails at the first
// task needing the new package with an R error about a missing library rather than anything about
// the image.
//
// REGIONAL QUOTAS THAT CAP CONCURRENCY -- READ BEFORE SETTING executor.queueSize
//
// A full sweep is 6,000 POWER_SIMULATION tasks, and Nextflow submits each one as its own Google
// Batch job. Three different regional quotas bind before any CPU quota does. All three were found
// the hard way, one failed launch at a time, on 2026-09-05 in us-central1. Batch does not surface
// them in Nextflow's log; it reports CODE_GCE_QUOTA_EXCEEDED in the JOB's status events, so the
// way to diagnose a stalled run is:
//
//   gcloud batch jobs describe <job> --location <region> --format=json   # then read statusEvents
//
//   LOCAL_SSD_TOTAL_GB_PER_VM_FAMILY  30,000 GB  -> 80 concurrent tasks, IF FUSION IS ENABLED
//       Fusion attaches a 375 GB local SSD to every VM, and 30000/375 = 80 exactly. This is the
//       one that hurts: it silently capped a sweep at 80 tasks and a ~47 h wall clock, long enough
//       to outlive the ephemeral container image the run depended on. Fusion earns nothing for this
//       workload -- measured staging is ~109 MB per task, and the largest single input in the DAG
//       is the 428 MB object PREPARE_SIM_INPUT reads once -- so the fix is to run on a compute
//       environment with Fusion DISABLED (`hgrm-cloud-batch-us-central-WIF-nofusion`).
//
//       DO NOT try to disable it per-run with `fusion.enabled = false` against a Fusion-enabled
//       compute environment. It half-applies: the local SSD goes away but Seqera still generates
//       Fusion-style /mnt/disks/<bucket>/... paths, so .command.run resolves into a mount backed by
//       nothing and every task dies with "Too many levels of symbolic links". The compute
//       environment wins; only the compute environment can turn Fusion off.
//
//   REGIONAL_INSTANCE_GROUP_MANAGERS  2,000     -> ~2,000 concurrent tasks
//       Batch creates one Managed Instance Group per job, so this is a hard ceiling on in-flight
//       tasks regardless of how much CPU is available. Other workloads in a shared project consume
//       some of it (~300 when measured), so the usable figure is lower than the limit. Setting
//       queueSize above it is not fatal -- the surplus churns through Batch's three internal
//       retries and Nextflow's errorStrategy -- but it is wasted work.
//
//   IN_USE_ADDRESSES                  2,300     -> ~2,300, only if usePrivateAddress = false
//       Not yet reached in practice, because MIGs bind first. Switching to private addresses to
//       dodge it would be a false economy: the VMs would then need Cloud NAT to pull the container
//       image, and ~6,000 pulls of a 0.68 GiB image through a NAT gateway costs far more than the
//       external IPs do. It only becomes worthwhile if the image moves to Artifact Registry, which
//       Private Google Access can reach without NAT.
//
// CPUS and PREEMPTIBLE_CPUS were never the constraint: 5,000 and 10,000 respectively, against ~120
// in use. Do not waste a quota request on them.
//
// So: queueSize ~1,700 is the honest setting for this region today. Raising
// REGIONAL_INSTANCE_GROUP_MANAGERS is what unlocks more.

params {
    container_image = params.container_image ?: null
}

process {
    container    = params.container_image
    beforeScript = {
        "mkdir -p \$(dirname ${projectDir}) 2>&1 || true; " +
        "rm -rf ${projectDir} 2>&1 || true; " +
        "ln -sfn /opt/pipeline ${projectDir} 2>&1 || true"
    }
}

google {
    batch {
        spot = true

        // Let Batch itself retry a preempted task before Nextflow ever sees a failure. That is
        // cheaper than a Nextflow-level retry, which re-stages inputs and re-enters the queue, and
        // it keeps preemption out of the run's error statistics where it would look like breakage.
        //
        // This does NOT replace process.errorStrategy in conf/base.config. Spot retries handle
        // preemption; errorStrategy handles everything else, including the case Batch cannot see --
        // a task killed before it writes .exitcode, which arrives with no exit status at all.
        maxSpotAttempts = 5
    }
}

process {
    // Ask for the exact shape rather than letting a standard VM be over-allocated. Only
    // POWER_SIMULATION is pinned, deliberately: it is 6,000 of the ~6,120 tasks and ~99 % of the
    // bill, so it carries essentially all of the saving, while a wrong machine type on any of the
    // others would block a run for nothing.
    //
    // `c2d-highcpu-2` (2 vCPU / 4 GB) rather than a custom N1/E2 shape: predefined C2D pricing
    // comes out cheaper than an equivalent custom shape, and C2D's per-vCPU throughput is higher,
    // so the same 4 GB budget now comes with a second vCPU at lower cost rather than at a premium.
    // 16 GB on this shape's 2 vCPUs -- which CONSOLIDATE_REPLICATES would need -- is still out of
    // range, which is why the high-memory processes are left for Nextflow to derive from their
    // cpus/memory directives, where it can pick a legal shape itself.
    //
    // Note the 4 GB in conf/base.config was chosen for SHERLOCK's scheduler, not GCP's: `owners`
    // allocates 4 GB per core, so a 6 GB request needed a node with spare memory rather than a
    // spare core. Measured peak RSS is 2.27 GB median / 3.27 GB max, so the figure is honest on
    // either platform -- but if GCP pricing ever favours a different shape, this is the knob.
    withName: POWER_SIMULATION {
        machineType = 'c2d-highcpu-2'
    }
}
